\description{
Estimates treatment effects (differences from a reference group) via
survey-weighted regression. Supports bivariate and multivariate models,
Gaussian and non-Gaussian families, and optional subgroup analysis.
}
\details{
\subsection{Estimation Paths}{

\code{get_diffs()} uses two estimation paths:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Clean path} (bivariate Gaussian with no covariates and no group, OR
any family with \code{scale = "link"}): extracts coefficients directly from
\code{clean()}. The intercept is the reference group mean; treatment
coefficients are differences from reference. When \code{scale = "link"} and
the family is non-Gaussian, \code{mean} and \code{pct_change} are suppressed.
\item \strong{Marginaleffects path} (covariates, non-Gaussian with
\code{scale = "ame"}, or group): uses \code{avg_slopes()} for estimates and
\code{avg_predictions()} for means.
}
}

\subsection{Link-Scale Suppression}{

When \code{scale = "link"} and the family is non-Gaussian, the \code{mean} and
\code{pct_change} columns are suppressed (omitted entirely). Link-scale
means are not substantively meaningful.
}

\subsection{P-Value Adjustment}{

When \code{group} is active, p-value adjustment is applied independently
within each group. For global adjustment across all comparisons,
apply \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} to the result manually. Confidence intervals
reflect the specified \code{conf_level} and are not affected by p-value
adjustment.
}

\subsection{Degrees of Freedom}{

All p-values and confidence intervals use the t-distribution with
design-based residual degrees of freedom, regardless of estimation
path.
}

\subsection{Non-Gaussian Models}{

By default, non-Gaussian models report average marginal effects on
the response scale. Set \code{scale = "link"} for coefficients on the link
scale (e.g., log-odds for logistic regression).
}
}
